\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 With MongoDB alone, it\hich\f1 \rquote \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 
s difficult to keep one saved document consistent with the next. Mongoose offers tools to build models with schemas defining what type of data can be saved.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Use Mongoose (Structured Data)}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\ul\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Example 1: E-commerce Store}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 You're building an online store. Every product should have:
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Name (required)
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Price (required, must be a number)
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Description
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Category
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Stock quantity
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Images
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Why Mongoose? You want to enforce that every product has a name and price. You don't want someone accidentally saving a product w\hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 
ithout a price, or with the price as text instead of a number. The structure is predictable and consistent.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\ul\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Example 2: User Registration System
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Every user must have:
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Email (required, must be unique and valid format)
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Password (required, hashed)
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Username (required)
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Created date (auto-generated)
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Why Mongoose? You need validation - emails must be in proper format, usernames must be unique, passwords must exist. Mongoose handles all this validation automatically.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Use MongoDB Native Driver (Flexible Dat\hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 a)}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\ul\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Example 1: Logging System}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 You're collecting logs from different parts of your application:
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Error logs might have: \{timestamp, error, stackTrace, userId\}
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - API logs might have: \{timestamp, endpoint, method, responseTime\}
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - User activity logs might have: \{timestamp, action, deviceInfo, location\}
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Why Native Driver? Each log type has completely different properties. Trying to create a schema that fits all of them would be messy. Just store whatever data comes in.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\ul\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Example 2: Analytics/Events Tracking}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 You're tracking user events on a website:
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Button click: \{event: "click", button: "checkout", timestamp, userId\}
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Page view: \{event: "pageview", page: "/products", duration, timestamp\}
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 - Purchase: \{event: "purchase", items: [...], total, paymentMethod, timestamp\}
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Why Native Driver? Every event type has\hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 
 different properties. The data is unpredictable and constantly evolving as you add new event types. You just want to dump the data in and analyze it later.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Creating a Schema
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 
A schema is like a class definition in some languages or, more broadly, a blueprint for how you want data to be organized for specific objects in your application. Avoid inconsistent data and specify the structure of your data.
\par 
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 Schema class from mongoose package allows us to create an instance of the class or create a Schema object. \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 
With this object/instance we have access to methods from the Schema class like .pre(save) hook and built in validation capabailities.
\par \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 From the schema object we can create a model (the factory / constructor / builder). We use that model to create new documents based on the schema.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\ul\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\lang9\langfe7177\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8134126 \hich\af1\dbch\af31505\loch\f1 The Flow
